  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 311 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat and humidity will persist from today and tomorrow. A \nlow chance of afternoon and evening showers and thunderstorms \ncontinues Sunday, staying primarily dry Monday but cannot rule \nout a few showers for the southern extent of the Hampton Roads \narea/Northeast North Carolina.\n\nAn offshore subtropical ridge continues to prevail as a trough \nfrom the Great Lakes is moving into the Northeast, leading to \nzonal flow for the area today and tomorrow. At the surface, an \noff- shore high pressure system persists, leading to hot and \nhumid conditions for the area today and tomorrow. With dew \npoints struggling to mix out, daily high temperatures will warm \nup to the mid-90s and dew points will stay in the low-mid 70s. \nThe combination of heat and humidity will lead to heat indices \nto rise into the upper-90s/low-100s with some localized areas in\nSouthside Hampton Roads into areas of North Carolina between \nthe Chowan River and the Albemarle Sound having heat indices \nreaching around 105.\n\nIn terms of storm chances for today, the combination of a \nbay, river, and sea breeze later this afternoon may lead to \nshowers and thunderstorms for coastal Northeast North Carolina \nand coastal Virginia later this afternoon into this evening. In\naddition, there is also a slight chance of pops for the piedmont\nas storms may fire off the high terrain and Lee Trough for this\nafternoon/evening. High- res models show signals of a few \nshowers/thunderstorms occurring along the coast, but there \nremains uncertainty on placement. The primary risk for any \ncells that develop are localized flooding and lightning. The\nweather pattern looks to remain consistent for Monday as the\nheat and humidity persists with highs in the low to middle 90s\nand heat indices hovering the upper 90s to low 100s with some \npotential of 105F heat indices across the far SE. Storm chances\nlook minimal Monday, however, a stray shower and or \nthunderstorm cannot be ruled out with a for parts of northeast \nNorth Carolina from the late afternoon into the evening.\n\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled weather pattern looks to return to \nthe area Tuesday and thursday in addition to the heat and humidity \nremaining in place. \n\nThe recent 00z/09 ensembles are in decent agreement on the upcoming \nsynoptic weather pattern. The zonal flow pattern is expected to \nbreak down as a ridge aloft starts to build in Tuesday across the \ncentral United States. This will allow for WNW flow aloft to return \nto the area. While at the surface, the high Bermuda high will start \nto break down slightly and move over the the Gulf states. This will \nallow the possibility for multiple days of  active weather (Tues-\nThurs) to return to the area. However, there still remains quite a \nbit of uncertainty at this time due to some slight disagreement in \nthe models in the lowest levels of the atmosphere. However, the \nsynoptic pattern does look favorable. In addition to the storm \nchances, the heat and humidity will persist Tues-Thurs. The best day \nfor potential Heat Headlines continues to remain Tuesday as his \nlooks to look like the hottest day of the week with middle 90s. At \nthis time not expecting dew points to mix and should remain in the \nlow to middle 70s. The highest confidence in potential Heat Headlines \nlooks to be across the SE coast where cloud clover looks to be less. \nFor Wed and Thurs temperatures will potentially be in the  low to \nmiddle 90s but depending on convection and cloud cover they could be \nslightly cooler. That is why at this time confidence in potential \nHeat Headlines these days are low. Nonetheless, Heat Indices will \npotentially be in the low 100s. By the end of the week, ensembles \nhint on a cold front pushing through the area that could help lower \ntemperatures and dew points across the area.",
